# Copy to `.env.local` (git-ignored) and fill in. Without this, everything works
# exactly as before — the sync button simply says the build isn't configured.

# Google OAuth client for syncing to the user's own Google Drive.
#
# In the Google Cloud console:
#   1. APIs & Services → Library → enable "Google Drive API".
#   2. Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID.
#   3. Application type: *Web application*. Not "Chrome Extension" — that type
#      only works with chrome.identity.getAuthToken, and Google rejects the
#      chromiumapp.org redirect for it.
#   4. Authorized redirect URIs → add, exactly, including the trailing slash:
#        https://<YOUR_EXTENSION_ID>.chromiumapp.org/
#      Load the unpacked build once to read the id from chrome://extensions. To
#      keep it stable between reloads, add a "key" to the manifest.
#   5. OAuth consent screen → add only the scope
#        https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.appdata
#      This is the "app data" scope: a hidden folder only this extension can
#      read. It gives no access to anything else in the user's Drive.
#
# The phone build (`yarn build:web`) shares this one client, and needs one more
# thing on it: Authorized JavaScript origins → the site's origin, e.g.
#   https://<username>.github.io
# Origin only, no path. That build signs in through Google Identity Services,
# which validates the origin and uses no redirect URI at all.
#
# The client id is not a secret — it ships in the bundle either way. There is
# deliberately no client secret here: a public client can't keep one.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# Pins the extension id, so the one redirect URI registered above keeps working
# after every reload — and so every tester's install shares that same id.
#
# Two places it can come from, and they mean different ids:
#
#   * `yarn key` generates our own key pair and fills this in. Use this while
#     handing the extension round as a zip; nothing needs to exist yet.
#   * The store item, once there is one: dashboard → the item → Package tab →
#     View public key, pasted here (newlines are stripped, so a multi-line paste
#     on one line is fine). Switch to this to match the published id.
#
# A public key, not a credential. `yarn package` removes it from the store zip,
# which refuses uploads that declare their own key; `yarn package:testers` keeps
# it, because that is the whole point of a tester build.
LUGIN_EXTENSION_KEY=

# Where the extension reads card prices from, to value a collection without a
# request per card. Point it at prices.json beside the deployed phone app, e.g.
#   https://<username>.github.io/lugin/prices.json
# The Pages deploy rebuilds that file daily from Scryfall's bulk dump (see
# scripts/build-prices.mjs) and serves it with `access-control-allow-origin: *`,
# so no host permission is involved.
#
# The phone build ignores this and always reads prices.json next to itself. Leave
# it empty and the extension simply shows no collection value.
VITE_LUGIN_PRICES_URL=

# Phone build only: URL of the MTGGoldfish CORS proxy (workers/goldfish-proxy.js).
# Deploy once with `yarn goldfish:deploy`, then set this to the worker URL, e.g.
#   https://lugin-goldfish.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev
# For local phone dev (`yarn dev:web`), run `yarn goldfish:proxy` in another
# terminal — the app defaults to http://127.0.0.1:8787 when this is empty.
# GitHub Pages: set this repository variable, or add a CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN secret
# so the deploy workflow publishes the worker automatically.
VITE_LUGIN_GOLDFISH_PROXY_URL=
